WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.062
A cleaned value should be explainable.

00:00:02.062 --> 00:00:07.467
In these examples, we remove formatting noise and apply a documented abbreviation rule.

00:00:07.467 --> 00:00:10.017
We are not inventing new information.

00:00:10.017 --> 00:00:17.037
Keeping raw and cleaned values side by side is useful during development because it makes every transformation

00:00:17.037 --> 00:00:21.402
visible. That helps catch over-cleaning and makes the process easier to audit.

00:00:21.402 --> 00:00:26.217
Connect this idea to an operations team aligning timestamps across locations.

00:00:26.217 --> 00:00:31.072
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:31.072 --> 00:00:37.987
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:37.987 --> 00:00:43.057
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:43.057 --> 00:00:48.240
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
